          You can connect a generator to your house in exactly four legal ways: extension cords run straight to the appliances, a manual transfer switch, an interlock kit with a power inlet box, or an automatic transfer switch. The first one you can do yourself today. The other three involve work inside the electrical panel, which means a licensed electrician and, almost everywhere, a permit and an inspection. The thing you must never do is plug a generator into a wall outlet or a dryer receptacle with a double-ended cord. That is not a shortcut; it is how line workers die.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="four-legal-ways" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Generator Hookup for Home: The Four Legal Ways
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every safe generator connection to a house shares one property: it is physically impossible for generator power and utility power to meet. Extension cords achieve that by never touching the house wiring at all. The other three methods achieve it with transfer equipment that disconnects the house from the utility before the generator can feed it. The National Electrical Code requires that transfer equipment for any generator connected to premises wiring, and that requirement is the reason the panel-connected options need an electrician and a permit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Which method fits depends on what you need to run. If your essentials are a refrigerator, a freezer, a few lamps and phone chargers, cords are enough and cost nothing beyond the cords. Once the list includes anything hardwired, such as a furnace blower, a well pump, a sump pump on a dedicated circuit or central air, you need a panel connection, because there is no plug to move to a cord. The choice between a transfer switch and an interlock is mostly about flexibility, budget and how your panel is laid out, and our <a href="/interlock-kit-vs-transfer-switch/">interlock kit vs transfer switch</a> comparison covers that decision in depth.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it powers</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Who installs</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Permit?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Extension cords to appliances</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plug-in appliances only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Homeowner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Manual transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A pre-chosen set of circuits, including hardwired ones</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electrician</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Almost always</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interlock kit + inlet box</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Any circuit in the panel, one at a time by choice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electrician</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Almost always</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Automatic transfer switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Selected circuits or the whole panel, switched automatically</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electrician</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Always</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Backfeed cord into an outlet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing safely</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nobody</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Illegal</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            This page is the procedural hub: what each route involves, the order of jobs, and the operating sequence once it is in. For hardware costs and the full catalogue of switch types, see our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types, cost and installation</a> guide. For the inlet box itself, see our guide to the <a href="/best-generator-inlet-box/">best generator inlet box</a>. Neither is repeated here.
          </p>

          <p>
            One planning note before choosing. Decide what the generator needs to carry first, then choose the connection, not the other way round. A 30-amp connection caps the generator at 7,200 watts and a 50-amp connection at 12,000, so the hookup can quietly limit the generator you already own. Our page on <a href="/what-can-a-10000-watt-generator-run/">what a 10,000 watt generator can run</a> works through that ceiling with real house loads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="backfeed-danger" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Backfeeding Kills Line Workers
          </h2>

          <p>
            A backfeed cord, sold online and known in the trade as a suicide cord, has a male plug on both ends. One end goes into the generator, the other into a household outlet or the dryer receptacle, and the generator energises the house wiring through the outlet. It appears to work. It is illegal in effect everywhere, it voids most homeowner insurance claims arising from it, and it creates three separate hazards.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first is to the people restoring your power. With the main breaker on, generator power flows out of your panel, up the service drop and into the utility transformer on the pole or pad. A transformer works in both directions. The one that steps distribution voltage down to your 240 volts will step your generator's 240 volts back up to the line voltage, commonly 7,200 volts or more on residential distribution. A line worker who has isolated a section of line and believes it is dead can be electrocuted by a neighbour's generator. Utilities warn about this every storm season for exactly that reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second hazard is to you. A cord with a male plug on each end has live, exposed prongs on whichever end is not plugged in. Plug the generator end in first and the other end is a bare 120 or 240 volts in your hand. The third hazard is to the house and the generator. If the main breaker is left on, or someone switches it back on, and utility power returns, the grid and the generator are suddenly connected out of phase. The result can be anything from a destroyed generator to a fire in the wiring, and household circuits were never designed to carry the full generator current through a single receptacle in reverse.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE NON-NEGOTIABLE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never plug a generator into a wall outlet or dryer receptacle.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a connection does not include a device that physically disconnects the utility before the generator can feed the house, it is a backfeed. Use cords to the appliances, or have an electrician install a transfer switch or interlock.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Every legal method on this page exists to make that out-of-phase meeting impossible, either by keeping the generator out of the house wiring entirely or by putting a mechanical or electrical lock between the two sources. If you take one thing from this page, it is that the shortcut has no safe version.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extension-cords" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Option 1: Extension Cords Direct to Appliances
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the method every generator owner can use on day one, and the only one that needs no electrician. The generator sits outside, cords run from its receptacles to individual appliances inside, and nothing touches the house wiring. It is entirely legal and, done properly, entirely safe. Its limits are that it only reaches plug-in appliances and that cords through a doorway or window let in weather and, if you are careless about placement, exhaust.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the homeowner does</h3>

          <p>
            Step one is placement. The generator goes outdoors, at least 20 feet from the house as the CDC recommends, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents, and never in a garage, even with the door open. Our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers distance, rain cover and theft. Step two is the cords: outdoor-rated, three-prong, heavy enough for the load and the length. A 12-gauge cord is the usual minimum for a refrigerator at 25 to 50 feet, and our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord size</a> guide has the full gauge and length tables.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step three is routing. Run cords through a slightly opened window or door with the gap sealed around them as best you can, or through a purpose-made cord pass-through port if you plan to do this often. Never run cords under rugs, through closed doors that pinch them, or across walkways where they become a trip hazard. Step four is load. Count the appliances on each cord and each generator receptacle; a typical 20-amp receptacle carries about 2,400 watts at most, and a cord carries only what its gauge allows. Plug in the largest motor load first and let it start before adding the next.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What cords cannot do</h3>

          <p>
            Cords cannot run anything hardwired. That includes most furnaces, well pumps, central air, many sump pumps, ceiling lights and built-in appliances. Some furnace installations can be fitted with a cord-and-plug connection or a dedicated transfer switch for the furnace alone, but that is electrician work to code, not a homemade pigtail spliced to the furnace wiring. If the list of things you need includes even one hardwired load, the cord method is a stopgap and one of the next three options is the real answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inlet-plugs-cords" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Inlets, Plugs and Cords: Matching 30A and 50A
          </h2>

          <p>
            Options 2, 3 and 4 all connect the generator to the house through the same chain: a 240-volt receptacle on the generator, a heavy four-conductor cord, and a power inlet box mounted on the outside wall that feeds the transfer switch or interlocked breaker. The inlet is a flanged male connector, which is precisely what makes it safe: the house side never presents live exposed pins, because the cord's female end plugs onto the inlet. Every part of the chain must carry the same rating, and the weakest part sets the ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are two common sizes. A 30-amp system uses the NEMA L14-30 twist-lock: an L14-30 receptacle on the generator, a cord with an L14-30 plug on one end and an L14-30 connector on the other, and an L14-30 inlet on the house. It carries up to 7,200 watts at 240 volts and is normally run on 10-gauge, four-conductor cord. A 50-amp system uses either the CS6365 California-style twist-lock connector, which mates with a CS6375 inlet on the house, or a straight-blade 14-50 at the generator. It carries up to 12,000 watts and is normally run on 6-gauge, four-conductor cord. Many generators with a 14-50R outlet use a cord with a 14-50 plug at the generator end and a CS6365 connector at the house end.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">System</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Generator outlet</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">House inlet</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Max watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical cord</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">30A, 120/240V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">L14-30R</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">L14-30 inlet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7,200W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10/4 (10-gauge, four conductors)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50A, 120/240V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14-50R or CS6369</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">CS6375 inlet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6/4 (6-gauge, four conductors)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">30A, 120V only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">TT-30R or L5-30R</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not for house panels</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,600W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10/3</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two rules follow. First, the cord, the inlet and the breaker it feeds are sized together by the electrician, and the generator's outlet determines which system you need; do not install a 30-amp inlet for a generator with a 50-amp outlet unless you accept losing the difference. Second, be careful with adapters. A listed adapter that lets a 30-amp generator feed a 50-amp inlet works because the generator's own breaker still limits the current, though you only get 30 amps. An adapter that feeds a 120-volt outlet into a 240-volt inlet is never acceptable: it leaves half the panel dead and can overload the neutral. Use the cord designed for the system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cord length matters less than people think at these gauges, but it still matters. Keep the generator-to-inlet run as short as your safe placement distance allows, typically 20 to 30 feet, and see the extension cord guide for voltage drop on longer runs. Our <a href="/best-generator-inlet-box/">inlet box guide</a> covers enclosure ratings, mounting and which inlets accept which cords.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="switch-or-interlock" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Options 2 and 3: Transfer Switch or Interlock, and Who Does What
          </h2>

          <p>
            A manual transfer switch is a small subpanel mounted beside the main panel. A selection of circuits, typically 6 to 10, are rerouted through it, and each has its own switch: utility, off or generator. An interlock kit is a sliding plate fitted to the main panel cover. It allows a dedicated generator breaker to be switched on only when the main breaker is off, so the whole panel becomes available to the generator and you choose which circuits to run by switching breakers. Both are fed from an inlet box outside. Both are legal transfer equipment when the device is listed or approved for your panel and installed under permit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The work divides cleanly between what a homeowner can and should do, and what belongs to a licensed electrician. Doing your half properly makes the electrician's visit shorter and cheaper and gets you a better result.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the homeowner does</h3>

          <p>
            Write the load list. Walk the house and list what you need during an outage, with running and starting watts from each appliance label or our wattage chart. That list tells the electrician which circuits belong on a transfer switch, or tells you which breakers you will switch on with an interlock. Identify the generator: note its 240-volt outlet type, its running watts and whether its neutral is bonded or floating, which the manual will state. Pick the inlet location, which should be on an outside wall near where the generator can sit safely 20 feet from openings, and as close to the panel as that allows. Photograph your panel label, make and model, because an interlock kit must be the one made or approved for that specific panel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the electrician does</h3>

          <p>
            Everything inside the panel and everything between the panel and the inlet. That means pulling the permit where required, mounting the inlet box and running cable from it to the panel, installing the transfer switch and rewiring the selected circuits through it, or fitting the interlock plate and installing the generator breaker with the retaining hardware the code requires for a backfed breaker. The electrician also checks that the panel has space and is in a condition worth adding to, balances the 120-volt circuits across both legs, and labels everything. We do not give step-by-step panel instructions here, because the panel's main lugs stay live even with the main breaker off, and that work is not a homeowner job.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Task</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Homeowner</th>
