                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Which to choose is a separate question, but as a hookup procedure the two differ in one important way. A transfer switch limits you to the circuits wired into it, which protects you from overloading the generator. An interlock gives you every circuit, which is more flexible and puts the job of not overloading the generator on whoever is flipping breakers. If you choose a switch, our <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">best manual transfer switch</a> guide compares the common 30-amp and 50-amp units.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="automatic-switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Option 4: Automatic Transfer Switches
          </h2>

          <p>
            An automatic transfer switch senses utility failure, signals the generator to start, waits for it to stabilise and then transfers the load, all without anyone touching anything. When the grid returns it transfers back and shuts the generator down after a cool-down. It is the standard on permanently installed standby generators and the most convenient option by a wide margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            With a portable generator it is a much harder fit. The generator must have a remote or automatic start capability compatible with the switch, the generator has to sit outdoors, fuelled and connected at all times to be useful, and it has to be protected from weather and theft while it waits. A few portables with electric start and an automatic start kit can do this, but most owners who want the convenience of automatic transfer end up concluding that a standby generator is the better answer. Our comparison of <a href="/portable-vs-standby-generator/">portable vs standby generators</a> sets out that trade.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a hookup procedure, the homeowner's part is mostly decisions: whole-house switch or a selected-circuit switch, and whether to add load management modules that lock out the air conditioner or water heater when the generator nears capacity. The installation is electrician work under permit, often with a gas permit too if the generator runs on natural gas or a propane line, and it is the most expensive of the four options. The cost guide linked above covers the numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Automatic switches do one more thing worth knowing: they remove the human error from the sequence. With manual equipment, the risk is forgetting a step. With an automatic switch, the only routine job is exercising the generator, which most standby controllers do on a weekly or monthly schedule by themselves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="permits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Permits, Inspection and the Utility
          </h2>

          <p>
            Assume a permit is required for any connection that touches the panel, because in nearly every jurisdiction it is. That covers the inlet box, the transfer switch and the interlock. The permit triggers an inspection, and the inspection is worth more than the fee: the inspector confirms the transfer equipment is listed for the application, the backfed breaker is secured, the conductors and breaker are sized together, and the inlet is a proper flanged inlet rather than a receptacle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some states and municipalities let homeowners pull a permit and do electrical work on their own owner-occupied single-family home. That is a legal route, not a recommendation. The work still has to meet code and pass inspection, the main service conductors in the panel remain live with the main breaker off, and a mistake in this specific job can energise the utility line. If you are not already competent at panel work, hire the electrician; this is one of the jobs where the fee buys real safety.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unpermitted generator connections cause problems long after the outage. Home inspectors flag them when you sell. Insurers can deny claims for fires traced to them. Some utilities ask to be told about a generator connection, and a few require it for standby installations; a call to yours costs nothing and settles the question. Keep the permit and the inspection sign-off with the house paperwork.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SIMPLE TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If it opens the panel, it gets a permit and an inspection.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Cords to appliances need neither. An inlet, a transfer switch or an interlock needs both in almost every jurisdiction, whoever does the work.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            While the permit is open is also the right time to ask the electrician to check two things that cause trouble later: whether your generator's neutral bonding suits the transfer equipment, which is the next section, and whether any GFCI or AFCI breakers in the panel will behave on generator power. Both are easier to settle on paper than during a storm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="neutral-bonding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Neutral Bonding: Floating vs Bonded
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every electrical system bonds its neutral to ground at exactly one point. In a house that point is the main service panel. Portable generators come one of two ways. A bonded-neutral generator connects neutral to its frame internally, which lets its own GFCI receptacles work when you run cords. A floating-neutral generator keeps neutral isolated from the frame, which is the configuration that suits a house connection through equipment that does not switch the neutral.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is why it matters. Most manual transfer switches and every interlock kit switch only the two hot legs. The generator's neutral is connected solidly to the house neutral, which is already bonded at the main panel. Connect a bonded-neutral generator that way and the system now has two bonds, so neutral current splits between the neutral wire and the ground path. The practical symptom is GFCI protection on the generator's 240-volt outlet tripping the moment the house load comes on. The less visible issue is current on ground conductors that should normally carry none.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are two correct fixes, and both are decided with the electrician rather than improvised. One is to use a floating-neutral generator, or one whose manufacturer documents how to float the neutral for house connection, with non-switched-neutral transfer equipment. The other is to use a transfer switch that switches the neutral too, which makes the generator a separately derived system that keeps its own bond. Do not remove a bonding jumper on your own unless the generator's manual tells you how and for what use, and remember that a floated generator used later with cords on a jobsite needs its bond restored, which many manufacturers handle with a bonding plug.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our guide to whether you <a href="/do-i-need-to-ground-a-portable-generator/">need to ground a portable generator</a> goes deeper on grounding electrodes, frame grounding and how to check which type you own. For this page the action is short: note your generator's neutral type before the electrician visits, and make sure the transfer equipment matches it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-sequence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. The Hookup Sequence During an Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once the equipment is in, the operating sequence is what keeps everyone safe, and it is the same whether you have a transfer switch or an interlock. Print it and tape it inside the panel door; a sequence remembered in daylight is easily muddled at 2 a.m. in a storm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Connecting</h3>

          <p>
            One: place the generator outdoors at its spot, at least 20 feet from openings, exhaust pointed away, and check the oil and fuel. Two: with the generator off and its main breaker off, plug the cord into the house inlet, then into the generator, and lock both twist-lock ends. Three: start the generator and let it run unloaded for a couple of minutes to settle its speed and voltage. Four: at the panel, switch off the main breaker, or with a transfer switch, move each circuit switch to off. Five: with an interlock, slide the plate and switch the generator breaker on; with a transfer switch, there is nothing to slide. Six: switch the generator's own 240-volt breaker on. Seven: bring circuits on one at a time, largest motor first, waiting for the engine to settle between each. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-a-portable-generator/">how to use a portable generator</a> covers load order and refuelling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Disconnecting when the power returns</h3>

          <p>
            You will know the grid is back when neighbours' lights come on or your utility status indicator, if your panel has one, lights up. Reverse the sequence. Switch circuits or the generator breaker off, so the house is off generator. Switch the generator's own breaker off. Slide the interlock back and switch the main breaker on, or move the transfer switches to utility. Let the generator run unloaded for a minute or two to cool, then shut it down and unplug the cord, generator end first. Never switch the main back on while the generator breaker is on; the interlock makes that physically impossible, which is the entire point of it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Step</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Connecting</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Disconnecting</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Place generator 20+ ft from openings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Confirm grid power is back</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Generator off: plug cord into inlet, then generator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Generator breaker in panel off (or switches off)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Start, run unloaded 2 minutes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Generator's own breaker off</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Main breaker off (or switches off)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Interlock back, main on (or switches to utility)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Generator breaker on (or switches to generator)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Run unloaded briefly, shut down</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">6</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Generator's own breaker on</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unplug cord, generator end first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">7</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Add circuits one at a time, largest first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Store cord dry and coiled</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Run the full sequence once on a calm day after installation, with the electrician present if possible, and again every few months when you exercise the generator. Owners who have practised it commonly report getting a house onto generator power in under ten minutes, and those who have not often spend that long finding the right breaker with a flashlight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a double-ended backfeed cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cord with a male plug on both ends energises the house through an outlet with no transfer device. It can electrocute line workers through the transformer, exposes live prongs, and destroys equipment when grid power returns. It is never acceptable, whatever an online listing calls it. Use appliance cords or a proper inlet and transfer equipment.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an interlock kit that does not match the panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Interlock kits are specific to panel brands and often to panel models, because the plate has to fit around the main and generator breaker positions. A generic or wrong kit may not pass inspection and may not actually lock out the main. Photograph the panel label first and let the electrician confirm the part.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing a 30-amp inlet for a 50-amp generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The connection caps the generator at the smaller rating. A 9,000 watt generator through an L14-30 inlet can deliver only 7,200 watts to the house, which is often the difference between running central air with a soft starter and not. Match the inlet to the generator's 240-volt outlet when the work is done.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring neutral bonding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bonded-neutral generator connected through an interlock or a non-switched-neutral transfer switch creates a second neutral-to-ground bond. The usual symptom is the generator's GFCI tripping instantly under house load. Check the generator manual for its neutral type before installation and choose the transfer equipment, or the documented floating option, to match.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the permit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An unpermitted inlet or interlock may work, but it gets flagged at sale, can undermine an insurance claim and has never been checked by anyone. The inspection confirms listed equipment, a secured backfed breaker and correctly sized conductors, which are exactly the details that matter during an outage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I connect a generator to my home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Either run outdoor-rated extension cords from the generator straight to individual appliances, which you can do yourself, or have an electrician install a power inlet box plus a manual transfer switch, an interlock kit or an automatic transfer switch. The panel-connected options need a permit in almost every jurisdiction. Never plug the generator into a wall outlet with a double-ended cord.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I hook up a generator to my house without a transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only through extension cords to individual appliances, or through an interlock kit, which is legal transfer equipment when it is approved for your panel and installed under permit. Any connection into the house wiring without a transfer switch or interlock is a backfeed, which is illegal and can electrocute utility workers and damage your equipment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a generator inlet box myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some jurisdictions allow homeowners to pull a permit and do electrical work on their own home, but the inlet connects to the panel, where the service conductors stay live even with the main breaker off. Unless you are already competent at panel work, have a licensed electrician do it under permit and get it inspected.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What cord do I need to connect a generator to an inlet box?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the generator's 240-volt outlet. A 30-amp L14-30 system normally uses 10-gauge, four-conductor cord with L14-30 ends. A 50-amp system normally uses 6-gauge, four-conductor cord with a 14-50 or CS6364 plug at the generator and a CS6365 connector at the inlet. Keep the run as short as safe placement allows.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it legal to plug a generator into a dryer outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A dryer receptacle is an outlet, not an inlet, so connecting a generator to it requires a double-ended male cord and creates a backfeed into the house and the utility line. It bypasses any transfer device. Even if the main breaker is off, the method is unsafe and not allowed under the electrical code.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to tell my utility I have a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Requirements vary. Some utilities ask homeowners to notify them about permanently connected generators, and some require it for standby installations. A quick call to yours settles it. What every utility agrees on is that the generator must be connected through transfer equipment so it can never energise their lines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far from the house should the generator be when hooked up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 20 feet from the house, as the CDC recommends, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents, and never inside a garage or under a carport roof. Plan the inlet location around that distance so the connecting cord can reach without the generator creeping closer to the house.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

