          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Hooking a generator to a house is a short list of decisions and one firm rule. Cords to appliances are legal and need nothing but the right gauge. Anything hardwired needs an inlet and a transfer switch, an interlock or an automatic switch, installed by an electrician under permit. A double-ended cord into an outlet is never the answer, because the danger lands on someone up a pole.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Do your half first: the load list, the generator's outlet and neutral type, the inlet location and the panel label. Then let the electrician do the panel and the inspector check it, and practise the sequence before you need it. If you are still choosing the generator itself, start with our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generators</a> roundup.
          </p>
        </div>

